<%
  /* The "Burial location" fact of a record card.
     Shared by the search cards (public/views/search.ejs) and the single-record
     page (public/views/grave.ejs) so the wording cannot drift between them.
     The source data carries no section or lot for essentially every record, so
     what the visitor is told about that has to be identical wherever it is
     shown — and it is deliberately not a claim about where the grave is.
     Call it inside a <dl class="record__facts"> with the record row, as an
     unescaped include:
         include('partials/fact-burial-location.ejs', { record: record })
     (That is the partial's only call form — see the two views for the tag
     around it; it is written without the delimiters here because a literal
     opening tag inside this comment would close it.)

     A record gets a "Burial location" row only when it genuinely carries a
     section or a lot. When it carries neither, the partial prints NOTHING —
     no row, no blank value, and no "not recorded" line — because the site
     cannot say where the grave is and must not appear to.

     __INVENTED_SECTIONS is a guard on known-bad data, not tidiness. The
     scraper (python-scraper/html_scraper.py, extract_grave_location) reads
     Find a Grave's "Plot info" line with a pattern that keeps the first run of
     letters it finds. For these cemeteries that line reads only
     "bamberg county memory" or "Marker only" — the only two Plot info lines in
     python-scraper/bcmg.html, and there are none at all in capernaum.html or
     honeyford.html — so the pattern invented a section out of a fragment of
     the cemetery's own name, out of the word "Marker", and once out of a stray
     year. Three of 2,428 records reached the database claiming a section this
     way: "BAMBERG", "MARKER", "2011". They are the only factually false lines
     the site has ever shown and must never be printed as a burial location.
     Any other stored value is rendered exactly as stored. The three came out
     of a case-insensitive regex, hence the upper-case comparison on a trimmed
     copy; the value that IS printed is the stored one, untouched. */
  var __locationRecord = (typeof locals !== "undefined" && locals && locals.record) ? locals.record : {};
  var __INVENTED_SECTIONS = ["BAMBERG", "MARKER", "2011"];
  var __locationText = (__locationRecord.section === null || __locationRecord.section === undefined) ? "" : String(__locationRecord.section).trim();
  var __isInventedSection = __INVENTED_SECTIONS.indexOf(__locationText.toUpperCase()) !== -1;
  var hasSection = !__isInventedSection && __locationText !== "";
  var hasLot = __locationRecord.lot !== null && __locationRecord.lot !== undefined && String(__locationRecord.lot).trim() !== "";
%>
<% if (hasSection || hasLot) { %>
<div class="fact fact--wide fact--place">
    <dt>Burial location</dt>
    <dd>
        <svg class="pin" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M10 1.5a6.5 6.5 0 0 0-6.5 6.5c0 4.6 6.5 10.5 6.5 10.5s6.5-5.9 6.5-10.5A6.5 6.5 0 0 0 10 1.5Zm0 9a2.6 2.6 0 1 1 0-5.2 2.6 2.6 0 0 1 0 5.2Z" fill="currentColor" />
        </svg>
        <span>
            <% if (hasSection) { %>Section <%= __locationRecord.section %><% } %>
            <% if (hasSection && hasLot) { %><span aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><% } %>
            <% if (hasLot) { %>Lot <%= __locationRecord.lot %><% } %>
        </span>
    </dd>
</div>
<% } %>
